rally do not acquire many  additional genetic lesions other than  the engineered mutations in contrast to  many human cancer types  as a result these tumors tend not to  recruit an endogenous anti-tumor t cell  response since there's very little  opportunity for the acquisition of  strong tumor neoantigens  in line with these observations the  pitet group at the massachusetts general  hospital showed that kc tumors do not  respond to immune checkpoint therapy  either in terms of survival on the left  or tumor burden on the right  however dr michael dupage a graduate  school colleague of mine who now has his  own lab at uc berkeley pioneered the use  of model neoantogens in the kp lung  cancer model  he called this the leukos model  expressing a chimeric protein consisting  of luciferase fused to of albumin and  cerebral antigens two commonly used t  cell antigen systems  michael engineered the lung tumors to  express these antigens by cloning them  into the lentiviruses used to initiate  tumors in this model  in this setting t cells do accumulate  within tumors as shown in the bottom  panels  however tumor-specific t-cells  progressively acquire features of  dysfunction in this model including  declining numbers and cytokine  production capacity as well as  expression of the suppressive checkpoint  molecule pd1  p-tech group showed that these  neoantigen-expressing tumors are capable  of responding to immune checkpoint  therapy when administered in combination  with so-called immunogenic chemotherapy  or auxaliplatin  based on these data we may predict that  the conventional kp model may more  closely mimic human lung cancers that  are unlikely to benefit from checkpoint  inhibition or non-t-cell inflamed  disease  on the other hand we might expect that  lung tumors engineered to express tumor  new antigens might better recapitulate t  cell inflamed disease  we sought to test this experimentally  additionally we set out to determine the  effects of administering engineered t  cell therapy on tumor immune phenotypes  in each of these two settings  in order to test this we turned to  clinical data sets generated by our  colleagues in the laboratory of dr  mcgarry houghton at fred hutch  the houghton lab performed unsupervised  hierarchical clustering of human  non-small cell lung cancers based on  immune cell profiling by split cytometry  they found that these cancers clustered  into four groups  first inert with very few immune  infiltrates of any kind second  myeloid dominated by myeloid subsets  primarily driven by neutrophils  third active characterized by high  levels of t-cell infiltration similar to  t-cell inflamed cancers and fourth  indeterminate with no clear pattern of  immune infiltration identified but  characterized by high levels of th17  type cytokines  the hoten group also identified an  immune signature that was elevated in  the active human lung cancer subset  including genes involved in cytotoxic t  cell responses and interference gamma  signaling  to perform a similar analysis on our  genetically engineered mouse model  tumors we generated kp tumors with or  without the expression of neoantigens  using the leukos system and with or  without administered engineered t cell  therapy and subjected these tumors to  transcription analysis using  nanostring's encounter pan cancer io 360  panel  we compiled a list of mouse genes  corresponding to the hoten active immune  signature that were present in our  analysis panel and assess the expression  of these genes by each kp tumor type  we observed that untreated tumors shown  in yellow express relatively low levels  of active immune signature genes  interestingly both the introduction of  tumor neoantigens shown in gray and the  administration of engineered t-cell  therapy shown in purple boosted active  signature gene expression  and neoantigen expressing tumors that  also receive t-cell therapy shown in red  were the most enriched for the signature  these data suggest that neo  expression and engineered t-cell therapy  are both able to skew kp tumors toward  an active immune phenotype  next we utilize the end solver software  to perform cell type profiling based on  the transcripts present in each sample  interestingly the untreated tumors shown  in yellow and gray clustered together  regardless of neoantigen expression  whereas the treated tumors shown in  purple and red clustered separately  focusing on predicted t cell infiltrates  similar to the active immune signatures  the untreated kp tumors in yellow showed  the lowest t-cell score while the  neo-antigen-expressing tumors that  received t-cell therapy in red scored  the highest and the tumors that  expressed either  either expressed neoantigens in gray or  received t-cell therapy in purple fell  in the middle  next we wanted to establish which human  non-small cell lung cancer subset is  best recapitulated by kp tumors at  baseline  focusing on these tumors in yellow we  observe that they are not devoid of  immune cells as measured by cd45 score  in the top left spot  however they were not particularly  enriched for either t cells or myeloid  subsets shown in the other three plots  digging deeper we analyzed the  expression of genes that the houghton  study found to be associated with either  the indeterminate group or the myeloid  group  compared to treated tumors the untreated  kp tumors were enriched for expression  of th17 cytokines associated with the  indeterminate class of human lung  cancers  but not with myeloid class associated  genes  thus we concluded that the kp lung tumor  model most closely recapitulates the  indeterminate class of human non-small  cell lung cancer  so in summary the second part of my talk  i've shown you that kb lung tumors  require few mutations harbor few t-cell  infiltrates and a recalcitrant  checkpoint inhibition similar to human  non-small cell lung cancers that do not  benefit from immune checkpoint therapy  introduction of model neoantogens as  shown by michael dupage's work increases  t-cell infiltrates and renders tumors  sensitive to immune checkpoint therapy  as shown by the ptet lab in combination  with immunogenic chemotherapy  cell type profiling and active immune  signatures indicate that kp lung tumors  most closely resemble the indeterminate  class of human non-small cell lung  cancers  and neoantigen expression and or the  administration of engineered t-cell  therapy remodels the tumor  microenvironment to a more active  phenotype  following on this work we're performing  single cell expression analyses to  determine heterogeneity within the lung  tumor microenvironment and therapeutic  t-cell substrates and changes in  phenotypes within these cells with  therapy  these observations allowed us to ask if  t cell therapy is capable of remodeling  the tumor micro environment to a more  active phenotype then why does this  therapy ultimately fail in this setting  one important reason why we believe  t-cell therapy does not provide  long-term benefits in this system is  that the therapy of t-cells appear to  lose function very rapidly in the lung  tumor micro-environment  in this assay we harvested therapeutic  t-cells from either the lung or from the  spleen as a control tissue seven days  after infusion into the animals  we stimulated them with mesothelioma  peptide in culture and measured their  capacity to produce cytokines  even at this very early time point after  infusion t cells harvested from the  lungs had significantly less cytokine  production capacity compared to those  coming from the spleen  interestingly though t-cell therapy  significantly prolongs survival in both  the lung and pancreatic cancer models  shown at the top  t-cells seem to lose function much more  rapidly in the lung tumor  microenvironment compared to the  pancreas tumor microenvironment as  measured by the same exterior  stimulation i previously described shown  in the bottom two graphs  this might help explain why the survival  extension in the lung cancer model which  is only about two and a half weeks is  more modest than that seen in the  pancreatic cancer model which is about  six weeks and suggests the existence of  tumor or tissue type specific factors in  the suppression of therapeutic t cells  interestingly we observed that this loss  of function is linked to antigen  exposure that is when we infuse t cells  specific to a control antigen that is  not expressed in the tumor  microenvironment here labeled as ot1 in  blue into tumor varying animals and then  stimulate them ex vivo with their  cognate antigen they do not lose  function to the same extent as the  mesothelion specific t cells shown in  red  that is unless we program the tumors to  express the control antigen in which  case these t cells do lose function to a  similar extent as the mesothelion  mesothelion-targeted t-cells  delving deeper into potential mechanisms  of t-cell suppression we perform bulk  transcriptional analysis on therapeutic  t-cells isolated either from the lung  tumor microenvironment or the spleen of  the controlled tissue in mysparine kp  tumors with or without model neoantigens  utilizing nanostrings encountered t cell  exhaustion panel  using the end solver software principal  component analysis revealed that the  therapeutic t cells clustered primarily  according to the tissue from which they  were harvested regardless of whether  the tumors expressed neo-antigens or not  pathway analysis revealed stark gene  expression changes occurring in the lung  tumor microenvironment  we saw the enrichment of genes  associated with t cell or t cell  receptor activation and altered  metabolism in the lung tumor  microenvironment compared to the spleen  consistent with ongoing antigen-induced  t-cell responses in this tissue  cytokine signaling signatures were also  upregulating upregulated highlighting  the complex immunology encountered by  therapeutic t cells in this setting  gene sets associated with t cell  function dysfunction such as energy  apoptosis senescence and quiescence and  t cell exhaustion were enriched in the  lung tumor microenvironment and cell  cycle pathway genes were decreased  possible contributors to t cell  dysfunction identified by pathway  analysis where hypoxic stress inhibitory  cytokines such as il-10 and tgf-data  signaling  checkpoint pathways and terminal  differentiation or exhaustion as  measured by epigenetic modification  to date we've only just begun to scratch  the surface in determining the relative  contributions of each of these pathways  and contributing to t-cell dysfunction  however one logical strategy for  boosting anti-tumor t-cell function  in tumors is the use of immune  checkpoint therapy  indeed we do observe increased  expression of checkpoint receptors on  therapeutic t cells from the long tumor  micro environment compared to endogenous  t cells shown in gray and we observe  that kp tumor cells do express  checkpoint ligands as shown on the  bottom  indicating that these pathways might be  active in the kp model in the context of  t-cell therapy  however when we administered immune  checkpoint therapy blocking all three of  these checkpoint pathways alongside  t-cell therapy  we did not observe any increase in  therapeutic t-cell function with  checkpoint therapy in blue compared to  t-cell therapy alone in red  in both cases t-cells harvested from the  lung exhibited similarly diminished  levels of function compared to those  coming from the spleen  these results indicate that checkpoint  blockade alone is not sufficient to  boost therapeutic t-cell function in  this setting and the other factors might  be acting to suppress t cell function  importantly others have shown that  inclusion of immunogenic chemotherapy  boosts the impact of checkpoint blockade  in similar animal models as i showed you  earlier highlighting the potential  importance of engaging innate immune  subsets  we have planned to test immune  checkpoint therapy in combination with  immunogenic genetic chemotherapy namely  auxiliary platinum in this setting  we're currently pursuing experiments in  which we can disrupt known suppressive  cell types that we have observed are  present in the kp lung tumor  microenvironment  for example by multiplex immunohistic  chemistry we observe the presence of  neutrophils here labeled in circles as  cd11b y6g double positive cells in and  around kp lung tumors after t cell  therapy  we also observe the presence of  regulatory t cells here labeled in  circles as cd4 fox p3 double positive  cells in close proximity with cytotoxic  t cells labeled with arrows  in the lung tumor micro environment  both of these cell types  t-regs and neutrophils have been shown  to inhibit anti-tumor t cell responses  in lung cancer and we have studies  underway in which we're depleting each  of these subsets to determine their  respective impacts on therapeutic t-cell  function  in the meantime we have begun to pursue  strategies for modifying therapeutic  t-cells to be resistant to suppressive  factors in the long-term  microenvironment  for example dr shannon oda a previous  postdoc in our lab who now has her own  group at seattle children's developed  immunomodulatory fusion proteins or ifps  these are chimeric molecules consisting  of the extracellular portion of  inhibitory receptors to use to the  intracellular portion of co-stimulatory  receptors such that binding to what  would normally be an inhibitory ligand  triggers a stimulatory  signal in the engineered t cell  for example  kp tumors express high levels of the  cd47 don't eat meat and innate immune  checkpoint ligand as shown in the bottom  right  shannon designed an isp that combined  the extracellular portion of the cd47  receptor surf alpha with the  intracellular signaling portion of the  cd28 t-cell co-stimulatory receptor  we found that inclusion of this isp in  our genetically modified t cells boosted  their function in the lung tumor micro  environment indicating that increased  co-stimulatory signaling can partially  overcome t-cell suppression in this  context  a second engineering strategy for  boosting t-cell function is the  inclusion of tumor-targeted helper  cd4-positive t-cells in our infusion  product  here we engineer cd4-positive t-cells to  recognize the same mhc class 1  restricted mesothelion antigen as our  conventional engineered cytotoxic cd8  positive t cells and we infused them  together into kp lung tumor varying  animals  we observed that inclusion of tumor  specific helper t cells boosted the  function of cytotoxic t-cells in the  lung tumor microenvironment indicating  that cd4 help can partially overcome t  cell suppression in this setting  so in summary of the last part of my  talk i've shown you that mesothelioma  targeted t-cell therapy does extend  survival in kp  lung cancer bearing animals but the  therapy of t-cells lose function rapidly  in the tumor micro-environment linked to  antigen exposure and leading to animals  to succumb to disease  immune checkpoint therapy alone fails to  boost therapeutic t-cell function in  this setting but we know that there are  suppressive cell types present within  the lung tumor microenvironment and we  have plans to test adjuvant therapies  targeting these populations  i've shown you some early experiments  testing strategies for boosting t-cell  function through engineering approaches  that have shown encouraging results and  i didn't have time to talk about this  today but our lab is also developing  cas9 based engineering approaches that  will allow us to more rapidly test the  effects of modulating candidate pathways  that are important for shaping t cell  phenotypes in the long-term micro  environment based on our immune  profiling data  so in conclusion of the talk i hope that  i've convinced you that t-cell therapy  does hold promise for patients who are  not likely to benefit from currently  available immunotherapies  genetically engineered balance models  are useful systems for evaluating the  efficacy of immunotherapies and for  elucidating the mechanisms of resistance  to therapies in tumor and tissue  specific contexts  however it's really important to  acknowledge the strengths and  limitations of genetically engineered  mouse models and to recognize the  relevance of these models to various  human disease settings  so with that i'd like to thank everyone  who was really instrumental to this  project including phil greenberg and all  the members of the greenberg lab both  past and present who were directly  involved in this project or who helped  me along the way  i'd also like to thank the university of  washington and fred hutch immunology  programs for all the useful help and  feedback  as well as my graduate lab the  laboratory of dr tyler jacks for many of  the reagents that you saw in today's  talk  i'd also like to thank the core  facilities of both the university of  washington and fred hutch as well as my  funding sources which are listed at the  bottom of the slide  with that i'd be happy to take any  questions if you just type them into the  the question  box and i i'd be happy to discuss this  work further with you so thank you very  much for your attention and have a great  day
 

